A Production-Ready Architecture

To make this enterprise-grade, structure the module around these services:

Service	Responsibility
Cryptographic Inventory Service	Continuously discovers algorithms, keys, certificates, libraries, and protocols across infrastructure.
Policy Decision Engine	Evaluates discovered cryptography against centrally managed policies and determines allow/deny/exception outcomes.
Approval Workflow Service	Manages multi-stage approvals (Security Architect, Compliance, CISO, etc.) with RBAC and workflow states.
Governance Ledger Service	Stores immutable governance records, receipts, signatures, timestamps, and audit chains in an append-only datastore.
Evidence & Reporting Service	Generates signed evidence packages, compliance reports, CBOMs, and auditor-ready exports.
Cryptographic Registry Service	Maintains the enterprise catalog of approved, deprecated, and prohibited algorithms, libraries, and providers.
Sophia Governance Copilot	Provides natural-language querying, explanations, policy recommendations, and governance insights based on authenticated tenant data.
The Differentiator

Many tools answer:

What cryptography exists?
Where is it used?

QGuard Helix can answer two additional questions that are often much harder:

Why is this cryptography allowed?
Can you prove that decision with cryptographically verifiable evidence?

That shifts the platform from being primarily a discovery and assessment tool to an enterprise Cryptographic Governance Platform. The strongest differentiator isn't simply using ML-DSA signatures—it's building a complete governance lifecycle where every cryptographic policy, approval, exception, change, and compliance artifact is tied to identity, time, authorization, and verifiable integrity. That provides organizations with defensible audit evidence and makes future algorithm migrations far easier because governance already exists above the cryptographic implementation itself.